blocks.content:
Every meaningful form of growth begins with understanding.

Before you can effectively build a career, business, organization, community or institution, you must first develop the ability to understand yourself.

Many people attempt to change their circumstances without understanding the person who is responsible for navigating those circumstances.

They acquire information without developing judgment.

They pursue opportunities without knowing their strengths.

They make decisions without understanding their values.

They enter relationships without understanding their behavioural patterns.

They choose careers without understanding their capabilities.

They start businesses without understanding their motivations.

They desire success without understanding what kind of person they must become to sustain it.

This module begins with the individual.

The objective is not to put the learner into a box or give them a permanent label.

The objective is to help the learner develop self-awareness.

Self-awareness means developing an increasingly accurate understanding of:

who you are;

how you think;

how you behave;

what you value;

what motivates you;

what you are capable of;

where you are weak;

what influences your decisions;

how you affect other people;

what you need to learn;

and who you are becoming.

The goal is not perfection.

The goal is accurate self-knowledge followed by intentional development.

By the end of this module, you should be able to:

explain what self-awareness means;

distinguish identity from personality, behaviour and circumstances;

identify your major strengths;

identify your development areas;

clarify your personal values;

examine beliefs that influence your decisions;

understand basic behavioural tendencies;

recognize the relationship between emotions and behaviour;

demonstrate greater personal responsibility;

identify areas where you need greater discipline;

distinguish healthy confidence from arrogance;

identify character qualities that need development;

recognize potential without confusing potential with achievement;

create a personal development profile;

create a practical personal growth plan.

Identity answers the question:

Who am I?

Identity should not be reduced to:

your job;

your salary;

your academic qualification;

your business;

your social status;

your possessions;

your current problems;

your successes;

or other people’s opinions of you.

These may describe parts of your current life, but they do not completely define your human capacity.

A person may currently be unemployed and still possess significant capabilities.

A person may have a degree but still have major development gaps.

A person may own a successful business but still need to develop leadership capacity.

Your current condition is not necessarily your final capacity.

Therefore, ask:

What kind of person am I?

What matters to me?

What do I naturally do well?

What do people consistently recognize in me?

What behaviours repeatedly create problems for me?

What kind of person do I want to become?

What capabilities must I develop?

A strength is an area in which you have comparatively strong natural ability, developed skill, experience, knowledge, character or capacity to produce useful results.

Strengths can include:

communication;

leadership;

creativity;

analysis;

organization;

technical ability;

teaching;

relationship building;

problem solving;

sales;

discipline;

resilience;

learning ability;

strategic thinking;

empathy;

execution.

A strength becomes significantly more valuable when it can be converted into value for other people.

Therefore:

Strength + Skill + Application = Value

Do not merely ask:

“What am I good at?”

Ask:

“What am I good at, and where can that capability solve a meaningful problem?”

A weakness is not a declaration that you are a bad person.

It is an area in which your current capability, behaviour or consistency is insufficient for the result you want to achieve.

Examples include:

poor time management;

weak communication;

lack of technical knowledge;

poor financial discipline;

inability to receive feedback;

procrastination;

emotional reactivity;

poor planning;

inconsistency;

lack of confidence;

inability to delegate;

weak digital skills.

There are several possible responses to a weakness:

develop it;

create a system that compensates for it;

collaborate with someone stronger in that area;

delegate it where appropriate;

avoid placing yourself unnecessarily in situations where the weakness creates unacceptable risk.

Maturity includes knowing the difference.

You do not need to become excellent at everything.

But you must know what you are weak at.

Values are principles or qualities that influence what a person considers important and how they make decisions.

Examples include:

integrity;

excellence;

freedom;

family;

service;

faith;

learning;

security;

creativity;

responsibility;

justice;

leadership;

growth;

contribution.

Values become particularly important when two desirable options compete.

For example:

You may want more money.

You may also value time with your family.

You may want rapid career advancement.

You may also value personal freedom.

Values help you determine what kind of success you are actually pursuing.

VALUE DISCOVERY EXERCISE

Choose your ten most important values.

Reduce them to five.

Then reduce them to three.

For each of the three, answer:

Why is this important to me?

What behaviour demonstrates this value?

Where am I currently violating this value?

What decision would I make differently if I lived this value consistently?

Motivation can start an action.

Discipline helps sustain it.

Self-discipline is the capacity to act according to important commitments even when immediate feelings do not support the action.

Examples:

studying when you would rather watch entertainment;

saving when you want to spend;

exercising when you feel lazy;

completing work before seeking distraction;

keeping promises;

arriving when you said you would;

continuing a worthwhile project after the initial excitement disappears.

Discipline is developed through repeated behaviour.

Start small.

Do not attempt to transform your entire life in one day.

Create one meaningful commitment.

Keep it.

Repeat it.

Increase the difficulty gradually.

Confidence is not believing that you can never fail.

Healthy confidence means having a realistic belief that you can learn, act, adapt and respond to challenges.

There is a difference between:

Confidence: “I can learn how to do this.”

and

Arrogance: “I already know everything.”

Confidence can grow through:

preparation;

competence;

experience;

keeping promises to yourself;

learning from failure;

receiving feedback;

taking progressively difficult challenges.

One of the strongest foundations of confidence is evidence.

Every time you make a meaningful commitment and keep it, you create evidence that you can trust yourself.

Character concerns the qualities that influence how you behave, especially when there is pressure, temptation or no external supervision.

Important character qualities include:

integrity;

honesty;

responsibility;

courage;

humility;

perseverance;

respect;

fairness;

reliability;

self-control.

Competence without character can become dangerous.

Character without competence can become ineffective.

The goal is:

Character + Competence + Capacity

A person should become both trustworthy and capable.

Potential is possibility, not achievement.

A person may have potential but fail to develop it.

Therefore:

Potential without development remains possibility.

Your potential can be explored through:

interests;

strengths;

learning speed;

curiosity;

experiences;

patterns of achievement;

feedback;

problems you enjoy solving;

responsibilities you handle well.

Do not ask only:

“What am I passionate about?”

Also ask:

“What problems am I capable of becoming excellent at solving?”

The world rewards useful capability.
